      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town began as a siding and water stop on the Southern Pacific Railroad, serving trains that crossed the San Pedro Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vail%2C_Arizona",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1930, sisters Esta and Lottie Trotter arrived to live in Vail and teach at the town’s first school, the Vail School.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.greatervailchamber.com/history-of-vail-az",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Vail Pass ski area, opened in 1962, was founded by Pete Seibert and local rancher Earl Eaton at the base of the pass.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
